<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Configuration>

  <!--
    The Windows Sandbox gate for a packaged Papyrus install.

    ## What this is for

    Everything else has been verified on a machine that has PHP, Java and Herd installed, with
    PATH scrubbed as a proxy for "nothing is installed". That is a good proxy and it is not the
    thing. A reader's machine has none of them, and this is the only check that can say so.

    ## Why the install is copied rather than run in place

    A mapped folder is read only to the guest, and the framework writes compiled packages and
    services into `bootstrap/cache` inside the application root. Running from the mount would fail
    on that, and the failure would say nothing about self containment. So the install is copied to
    `C:\Program Files\Papyrus` first, which is where a Windows installer puts one by default, so
    the paths the launcher and the application actually use are the awkward ones rather than the
    convenient ones.

    A genuinely read only install directory, as in Program Files, is a separate question and is
    not answered by this gate. It is called out rather than quietly assumed.

    ## Why there is a separate results folder

    The guest cannot write back to a read only mount, and a run whose output only exists inside a
    disposable machine is not much of a gate. Results go to a writable mapped folder so they
    survive the sandbox closing.

    ## The results folder must exist before this is launched

    Windows Sandbox will not create a mapped folder that is missing on the host. It fails to
    mount, the guest never reaches its logon command, and it closes with no error shown and no
    process left behind to look at.

    That is the worst failure this gate has: it looks exactly like a slow boot, so a watcher
    polling for a report sits there reporting "guest booting" for ten minutes. The folder is
    created by the host before launching. Delete build/sandbox-results to clear old results and
    the next launch silently does nothing.

    ## Do not edit the runner while a guest is booting

    The runner is read from this same mapped folder at logon, which is a minute or two after the
    window appears. Editing run-in-sandbox.ps1 in that window, or committing it, means the guest
    runs whatever the file happened to be when it opened it, or fails to parse it and closes with
    no progress file and no error.

    That happened twice in a row while working on this gate, and both times it looked like the
    machine was simply slow to start. Commit first, then launch, then leave both alone until the
    report appears.

    ## Only one guest at a time, and a closed window is not a finished one

    Windows Sandbox refuses to start a second instance while one is open, and it says so in a
    message that goes to a window nobody is watching. Launching again a minute or two after a
    previous run finished does not work either: the previous virtual machine has not released its
    resources yet, and the new one never appears.

    Three consecutive runs died this way, each with no progress file and no error, and each looked
    exactly like a slow boot. Close the window and wait for the guest process to go before
    launching again, and check for vmmemWindowsSandbox rather than trusting the clock.
  -->

  <VGpu>Disable</VGpu>
  <Networking>Default</Networking>

  <MappedFolders>
    <MappedFolder>
      <HostFolder>C:\Users\<you>\Php Projects\papyrus\build</HostFolder>
      <SandboxFolder>C:\build</SandboxFolder>
      <ReadOnly>true</ReadOnly>
    </MappedFolder>
    <MappedFolder>
      <HostFolder>C:\Users\<you>\Php Projects\papyrus\build\sandbox-results</HostFolder>
      <SandboxFolder>C:\results</SandboxFolder>
      <ReadOnly>false</ReadOnly>
    </MappedFolder>
  </MappedFolders>

  <LogonCommand>
    <Command>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\build\sandbox\run-in-sandbox.ps1</Command>
  </LogonCommand>

</Configuration>
